Geran-5 is a Russian jet-powered drone (6m long, 850kg takeoff weight, 90kg warhead, 1000km range, 500-600 km/h speed). 
It uses (for possibly lowest cost) a Chinese Telefly turbojet, carbon-fiber/composite airframe, commercial electronics (Chinese chips, mesh modems), 
and unified Geran parts. Low cost (around $50k after scaling) comes from simple design, COTS components, Chinese engines, 
and high parts commonality with cheaper Shahed/Geran family. Already in serial production : 500/month. Part of broader goal for tens of thousands of 
long-range drones yearly.

x15-x20 cheaper than cruise missiles like Kalibr or Flamingo. Designed for saturation attacks that need a lot of cheap mass.

The Geran-4 Seekers night AI vision combines infrared/thermal imaging cameras with onboard AI, allowing it to operate effectively in darkness, smoke, 
  and other low-visibility conditions. The system uses Dahua TiOC cameras that operate in both visible and infrared ranges, 
  paired with a computing platform that processes the video stream onboard. Machine vision algorithms then automatically detect 
  and highlight potential targets, enabling the drone to independently search, identify, and lock onto targets at night even without 
  direct operator control. Hence the drones name "Seeker". It can independently using only AI algorithms seek and destroy targets.

Geran-4 "Seeker" replaces simple coordinate attacks with terminal electro-optical guidance, giving it extremely high accuracy, often described as near-zero CEP,
  against fixed and moving targets. A secure video/data link allows a human operator to visually confirm the target and designate it in real time, 
  a concept known as "human-in-the-loop" targeting. The drone also uses AI-powered computer vision to independently search for, identify, and 
  lock onto targets without direct operator input, enabling autonomous terminal tracking to impact. The Seekers electro-optical sensor and machine-vision
  algorithms refine the aim point during the final dive, correcting for any drift. This allows it to hit targets with centimeter-level precision. 
  The terminal guidance system is designed for pinpoint accuracy against high-value targets such as vessels, port infrastructure, and railway equipment.

