Russians upgraded Geran-4 (again) :
An interesting change is the use of a new module referred to by Ukrainian intelligence as MOKH. 
  Its key element was the PNI5GA magnetometer manufactured by the American company PNI Sensor. 
  However, its problem is the accumulation of errors over the flight’s duration. 
  Even small measurement deviations can, after some time, translate into a significant difference between the UAV’s actual position and the position
  calculated by its onboard computer. A magnetometer can help mitigate this problem by providing additional information about the machine’s orientation
  relative to the Earth’s magnetic field.
The Russians have gone even further, as the new configuration also features the 'Susanin' system. This is an entirely different type of navigation: 
  instead of measuring the machine’s movement or using the magnetic field, the system utilises images from an optical camera. The computer compares 
  the currently viewed terrain with a previously saved reference image and, on this basis, can determine whether the UAV is on the correct route. 
  The system can correct navigation based on what it actually sees, rather than relying solely on GPS or inertial system data. According to Ukrainian
  military intelligence, a similar system has also been applied in the new Geran-5 in the K5 version.
Alongside the other changes, the Russians have also replaced the Geran-4’s power source. The Main Intelligence Directorate (HUR) reported that the 
  previously used Chinese Telefly turbojet engine has been replaced by a Russian equivalent, the production of which has been localised in Alabuga. 
  Ukrainian intelligence has not yet provided the designation of the new unit, its manufacturer, or exact parameters. It is known, however,
  that it is the same type of engine – a small turbojet unit.
